        {
          "id": "sec_assumptions_body",
          "component": "Text",
          "text": "This summary focuses on the original 1813 novel by Jane Austen and prioritizes the internal psychological development of Elizabeth Bennet over the subplots involving her younger sisters.",
          "variant": "body"
        },
        {
          "id": "divider_1",
          "component": "Divider"
        },
        {
          "id": "sec_plot_title",
          "component": "Text",
          "text": "Plot Summary",
          "variant": "h2"
        },
        {
          "id": "sec_plot_intro_h",
          "component": "Text",
          "text": "Introduction & Initial Prejudice",
          "variant": "h3"
        },
        {
          "id": "sec_plot_intro_b",
          "component": "Text",
          "text": "Elizabeth Bennet is introduced as a witty and independent young woman who prides herself on her ability to perceive the true nature of others. However, her arc begins with a significant failure of judgment when she encounters the wealthy Fitzwilliam Darcy. Offended by his social aloofness and a perceived insult to her appearance at a local ball, she develops a deep-seated prejudice against him. This bias is further reinforced by the charming but dishonest George Wickham, whose lies Elizabeth readily believes because they align with her existing dislike of Darcy, demonstrating her early reliance on first impressions and intellectual vanity.",
          "variant": "body"
        },
        {
          "id": "sec_plot_turning_h",
          "component": "Text",
          "text": "The Turning Point",
          "variant": "h3"
        },
        {
          "id": "sec_plot_turning_b",
          "component": "Text",
          "text": "The central turning point in Elizabeth’s development occurs following Darcy’s first marriage proposal, which she rejects with a scathing critique of his character and his interference in her sister's happiness. In response, Darcy provides a letter that clarifies his past actions and exposes Wickham’s true nature. Upon reading it, Elizabeth undergoes a painful process of self-examination. She realizes that her discernment was actually blindness fueled by her own pride. This realization is the most crucial part of her arc, as she acknowledges her own fallibility and begins to view Darcy through a lens of objective truth rather than personal resentment.",
          "variant": "body"
        },
        {
          "id": "sec_plot_res_h",
          "component": "Text",
          "text": "Resolution & Maturity",
          "variant": "h3"
        },
        {
          "id": "sec_plot_res_b",
          "component": "Text",
          "text": "In the final phase of her journey, Elizabeth’s character matures into a state of emotional and social balance. Her visit to Darcy’s estate, Pemberley, allows her to see his genuine character through the eyes of his servants and family, leading to a transformation of her feelings from hatred to respect and love. When Darcy secretly saves her family’s reputation following a scandal involving her sister Lydia, Elizabeth recognizes his true worth and her own previous folly. The arc concludes with her marriage to Darcy, symbolizing a union based on mutual respect and the successful overcoming of the internal obstacles of pride and prejudice.",
          "variant": "body"
        },
